N2  - Quantum Convolutional Neural Networks (QCNN) migrate the convolution and pooling concepts of classical Convolutional Neural Networks (CNN) to variational quantum circuits, showing potential on Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum (NISQ) devices. However, traditional QCNN adopts a single-depth circuit structure, which has limitations in multi-scale feature extraction, and is prone to the barren plateau phenomenon during training, making it difficult to adapt to high-dimensional real-world scenarios such as network intrusion detection. This paper proposes a QCNN architecture based on a multi-path parallel structure. For the network intrusion detection task, it draws on the Inception module to construct three parallel quantum convolution paths: shallow, medium, and deep, achieving hierarchical extraction of multi-scale features; residual connections are introduced to preserve encoded state information and provide gradient shortcuts; a hybrid optimization strategy combining classical Adam and Quantum Natural Gradient (QNG) is adopted to adapt to the heterogeneous parameter space. Experimental validation shows that, on the UNSW-NB15 network intrusion detection dataset, the proposed Multi-Path Parallel Quantum Convolutional Neural Network (MP-QCNN) improves detection accuracy by 3% compared to traditional single-path QCNN and effectively mitigates the barren plateau phenomenon. This design strictly maintains NISQ depth constraints and quantum parallelism advantages, providing an efficient and stable feature extraction framework for high-dimensional tasks such as network intrusion detection.


