N2  - Classical convolutional neural networks (CNNs) have achieved notable success in image classification but face challenges in scalability, interpretability, and computational cost. With the growing availability of Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum (NISQ) devices, hybrid quantum-classical models offer a promising alternative by leveraging quantum parallelism and entanglement. In this work, we present a hybrid Quantum Convolutional Neural Network (QCNN) that integrates quantum convolutional layers into classical image classification pipeline for the MNIST dataset. Our architecture features a modular, low-depth Quantum Kernel Convolution (QKC) design suitable for near-term hardware. We implement the system using Qiskit and evaluate in terms of classification accuracy, circuit efficiency, and integration feasibility. Our results demonstrate that (i) the hybrid QCNN achieves similar accuracy to classical models while reducing the number of classical parameters, and (ii) quantum layers can successfully perform dimensionality reduction through entanglement-based pooling. This study highlights the potential of hybrid QCNN as a practical quantum-enhanced model for real-world image recognition tasks.


