N2  - This paper presents a hybrid Quantum Convolutional Neural Network (QCNN) designed for handwritten character classification using the EMNIST dataset. The model combines quantum convolution and pooling circuits with classical neural network layers. Various ansatz and pooling strategies were explored, with the Conv10 and PoolG combination achieving the highest accuracy of 81.38 percent. Several quantum data encoding methods were tested, where angle encoding on the X-axis outperformed others. Additionally, optimization methods were compared, and the Adam optimizer showed the best performance in terms of convergence and stability. Although the model performs well overall, it encounters difficulties when distinguishing visually similar characters such as “0” and “0.” The results support the feasibility of applying hybrid QCNN architectures to image classification tasks and suggest future improvements in data representation and circuit design.


