N2  - Quantum Convolutional Neural Networks (QCNNs) represent an emerging paradigm that combines quantum computing principles with convolutional neural network concepts. However, limitations in dataset size and quantum processing complexity often hinder optimal performance. This research analyzes the effectiveness of various data augmentation techniques in improving QCNN algorithm performance, examining both pure quantum models and hybrid approaches with classical-to-quantum transfer learning. Data augmentation techniques are categorized into geometric transformations, photometric transformations, and their combinations. Experimental results demonstrate that augmentation significantly impacts full QCNN models, particularly when combined with angle encoding, achieving accuracy improvements up to 87.00 % with an average enhancement of 27.27 % compared to baseline without augmentation. For hybrid QCNN models, photometric transformations (P3) with saturation and hue adjustments achieved maximum accuracy of 73.00 % and average improvement of 12.93 % using ResNet18 architecture. These findings reinforce the potential of data augmentation as an important strategy in developing more accurate QCNN models.


