N2  - This paper investigates the effectiveness of classical and quantum-enhanced deep learning models for bone marrow cell classification, with particular emphasis on the role of quantum data encoding strategies. A classical EfficientNet combined with a convolutional neural network (CNN) is evaluated alongside multiple Quantum Convolutional Neural Network (QCNN) architectures employing single, dual, and multi-encoding schemes, including angle, basis, and amplitude encoding. The experimental results indicate that single encoding QCNN models achieve performance comparable to the classical baseline, with amplitude encoding yielding improved validation accuracy. Dual-encoding approaches demonstrate improved generalization, with the angle-amplitude encoding QCNN achieving the highest macro F1-score of 0.895 and a test accuracy of 89.25%. The multi-encoding hybrid QCNN further improves overall accuracy, attaining the highest training accuracy of 95.90% and test accuracy of 89.40%, though at the cost of increased model complexity. These findings highlight that carefully designed hybrid quantum encoding strategies can effectively balance representation capacity and generalization, offering competitive performance over classical models and underscoring the potential of QCNNs in Bone marrow cell classification.


