N2  - This study explores the application of Quantum Convolutional Neural Networks (QCNNs) in the realm of image classification, particularly focusing on datasets with a highly reduced number of features. We investigate the potential quantum computing holds in processing and classifying image data efficiently, even with limited feature availability. This research investigates QCNNs' application within a highly constrained feature environment, using chest X-ray images to distinguish between normal and pneumonia cases. Our findings demonstrate QCNNs' utility in classifying images from the dataset with drastically reduced feature dimensions, highlighting QCNNs' robustness and their promising future in machine learning and computer vision. Additionally, this study sheds light on the scalability of QCNNs and their adaptability across various training-test splits, emphasizing their potential to enhance computational efficiency in machine learning tasks. This suggests a possibility of paradigm shift in how we approach data-intensive challenges in the era of quantum computing. We are looking into quantum paradigms like Quantum Support Vector Machine (QSVM) going forward so that we can explore trade offs effectiveness of different classical and quantum computing techniques.


