N2  - Radio-based indoor localization is vital for the Internet of Things (IoT) applications such as smart healthcare. However, existing methods often require intrusive infrastructure or lack sufficient resolution for through-wall tracking. To address this, we propose a hybrid quantum-convolutional neural network-based framework (HQ-CNN) for high-precision localization in massive MIMO environments. The primary motivation for exploiting quantum-inspired techniques is to reduce model size and computational overhead through quantum data encoding, which is essential for resource-constrained IoT devices. Accordingly, the framework first extracts temporal channel state information, such as angle of arrival (AoA), to generate dynamic Angle-Delay Profiles (ADPs), capturing interactions with both static and moving objects. A generative adversarial network (GAN)-based frame prediction is employed for noise suppression and ADP refinement. The core innovation lies in the integration of a hybrid quantum-CNN, which improves learning from high-dimensional ADPs while maintaining low computational and memory overhead on a traditional GPU-based computer. Evaluations on DeepMIMO show the framework outperforms baselines in scalability and competes with their accuracy. This work demonstrates the potential of hybrid quantum-classical technologies for precise radio-based tracking.


