N2  - Quantum computing is fast transforming the medical world and giving it a new level of speed, accuracy, and safety in the health-care information. The latest developments use quantum machine learning and quantum-classical to improve medical imaging like classification, segmentation, and disease prediction, going beyond the capability of the classical algorithms. In the multifactorial disease cases, like cancer and neurological diseases, quantum-enhanced analytics will yield better clinical prognoses, better biomarker discoveries, and earlier detection. In the meantime, quantum-cryptography protocols are under development that will provide protection against privacy threats that are constantly changing to harm patient data. State-of-the-art quantum neural networks and adaptive hybrid quantum convolutional neural networks (CNNs) continue to optimize the process of diagnostics, reducing resource consumption, without losing the level of accuracy. This review maps these new applications, identifies the new trends, and talks about the future challenges of such applications, especially the requirement of explainable, interpretable quantum AI, which the clinicians can believe in. In brief, quantum technologies are in their infancy, but will have the ability to advance precision medicine, customised treatment and revolutionise medical decision-making.


