N2  - Potato leaf diseases pose a serious challenge to agricultural sustainability and food security in regions such as West Bengal, India, where potato cultivation plays a vital socio-economic role. Although classical deep learning models demonstrate strong performance, they often suffer from overfitting, limited generalization, and heavy reliance on large labeled datasets, while manual inspection remains time-consuming and error-prone. This work proposes a hybrid Quantum Convolutional Neural Network (QCNN) integrated with 4-qubit parameterized quantum circuits (PQCs) for accurate classification of five major potato leaf disease categories, including nutrient deficiency, fungal, and viral infections. The model incorporates quantum activation layers, quantum softmax, and a focused loss function to enhance non-linear feature representation and multi-class separability. Grad-CAM is employed to improve the interpretability of predictions. Experiments conducted on a diverse, field-collected dataset from Bardhaman, Bankura, and Purulia districts achieve a classification accuracy of 99.94%, demonstrating superior generalization, robustness, and explainability compared to classical approaches. This study highlights the practical potential of quantum-enhanced AI for precision agriculture.


