N2  - While Quantum Convolutional Neural Networks (QCNNs) show great potential for image classification tasks, their application is hampered by the lack of systematic guidance for hyperparameter configuration. The selection process, which is often a matter of trial-and-error, leads to suboptimal and inefficient model performance. This research addresses this issue by constructing an experimental framework to perform a comprehensive sensitivity analysis of QCNN hyperparameters. Using a constrained grid search method, thousands of model configurations were evaluated on the MNIST, Fashion-MNIST, and CIFAR-10 datasets under two scenarios: an ideal environment and an environment with a realistic quantum noise model. Analysis using non-parametric statistical tests reveals that nearly all tested hyperparameter categories, including the optimizer, embedding method, ansatz architecture, reduction method, and cost function, have a statistically significant impact on performance. The choice of optimizer and data embedding method were found to be the two most crucial factors. A key finding of this study is that the choice of hyperparameters actively and significantly determines the model's robustness to noise, revealing a clear trade-off between optimal performance in ideal conditions and resilience in noisy ones. This research provides a practical, evidence-based guide that can help researchers prioritize tuning to achieve more effective and efficient QCNN performance.


