N2  - One of the common and maybe fatal diseases harming women all around, breast cancer is one such condition. Accurate medical image analysis allows for early detection, which can greatly increase survival rates. Although they encounter challenges such as high computational expenses, poor data efficiency, and suboptimal feature extraction, conventional deep learning methods have produced promising results. By using quantum parallelism and entanglement to enable more quick and more complex computations, quantum computing provides a new approach to overcome these limitations. This paper intends to propose a hybrid quantum-classical deep learning model for the identification of breast cancer areas in mammographic images. The proposed method combines a quantum convolutional neural network (QCNN) with a classical convolutional neural network (CNN) to benefit from the advantages accompanying both classical and quantum processing. The hybrid model processes the mammogram data using classical pre-processing and quantum convolution layers; classification then follows with fully connected layers followed by classical pooling. The results of the MIAS dataset experiments indicate a classification accuracy of 98.4%, which is superior to the operation of traditional CNN and transfer learning-based models.


