N2  - In recent years, object detection technology has undergone substantial evolution within the realms of deep learning and computer vision, particularly owing to the extensive utilization of traditional convolutional neural networks (CNNs). These technologies perform well in a variety of applications, such as autonomous driving, security monitoring, and medical image analysis. However, traditional CNNS face many challenges when dealing with complex scenes and small target detection, such as background interference, target scale change and occlusion. In this paper, a novel Hybrid model, Hybrid Quantum-Cascade FPN, is proposed, which cleverly combines the advantages of Quantum convolutional neural network (QCNN) and classical CNN. The design of Hybrid quantum-Cascade FPN model is divided into three key stages: first, the Quantum coarse screening stage, which quickly extracts low-level features through quantum feature coding; Next is the quantum-classical feature fusion stage, which uses the parallelism of quantum computing and the structural advantages of classical networks to carry out multi-scale feature fusion. The last is the classical fine-tuning stage, which further optimizes the model to improve the high-level semantic analysis ability. Experiments on multiple data sets show that the model performs well in both detection accuracy and inference speed, especially in complex background and small target detection tasks.


