N2  - Magnetic Resonance Imaging (MRI) plays a pivotal role in modern medical diagnostics due to its non-invasive nature, superior soft tissue contrast, and ability to generate high-resolution anatomical and functional images. However, the in-creasing volume, dimensionality, and complexity of MRI datasets present significant challenges for traditional machine learning (ML) approaches, which often struggle with high computational costs, limited scalability, overfitting on small datasets, and re-duced interpretability. These limitations necessitate exploring alternative paradigms capable of addressing the computational and analytical demands of next-generation medical imaging. This review explores the integration of Quantum Machine Learning (QML) into MRI classification workflows, highlighting its potential to overcome several constraints of classical methods by leveraging quantum phenomena such as superpo-sition, entanglement, and parallelism. We provide an overview of quantum computing fundamentals and analyze prominent QML architectures-including Variational Quantum Classifiers (VQCs), Quantum Convolutional Neural Networks (QCNNs), Quantum Support Vector Machines (QSVMs), and Quantum-Enhanced Neural Networks (QANNs)-focusing on their design, computational efficiency, and classification performance. Notably, QML models such as QCNNs and QSVMs achieve up to 98 Finally, we discuss current challenges, open research prob-lems, and emerging opportunities in QML for clinical imaging, including quantum-enhanced compression, federated learning, and explainable AI frameworks. Our analysis demonstrates that QML provides significant advantages in accuracy, efficiency, and interpretability for MRI-based diagnostics, establishing a path-way toward next-generation quantum-driven healthcare systems.


