N2  - Brain-computer interfaces are based on analysis of Electroencephalograph (EEG) signals. Four critical brain patterns are taken into consideration for the same; namely Seizure, LPD (Lateralised periodic discharges indicating focal brain injury), GPD (Generalised periodic discharges associated with diffuse cerebral dysfunction), and LRDA (Lateralised rhythmic delta activity suggests structural lesions) Neurological conditions are diagnosed using insights from this analysis. However, standard ML models face difficulty in analysing the complexity of working on this type of data recorded in waveforms. Thus, there is a need for a more efficient and structured pathway. This research paper presents Quantum Machine Learning (QML) as a novel approach to analyse EEG signals in the field of neuroscience. The objective of this algorithm is to obtain higher accuracy while decreasing training and prediction time, and reduction in the number of false alarms. The study showcases a Hybrid Quantum- Classical Neural Network (HQCNN) applied on the Harvard Medical School (HMS) Harmful Brain Activity Classification dataset. HQCNN is a combination of Quantum feature encoding and classical classification algorithm. When this HQCNN was put to test, it achieved a 96.5 % accuracy rate. This clearly outperformed a classical Random Forest ML model. From the results obtained, it was inferred that HQCNN training is 1.8 times quicker. When it comes to making predictions, it was 57 times faster. This tells us the HQCNN performs well in both aspects, speed and precision. This makes it a better fit as a practical tool for tackling complex biomedical signals like EEG in this case. Hence, this HQCNN is a prime candidate for diagnosing conditions from much larger and more noisier datasets as well.


