N2  - Quantum Machine Learning (QML) fuses quantum computing with machine learning to address complex tasks with potential speedups over classical methods. While QML models such as variational quantum classifiers (QVC), quantum multilayer perceptron(QMLP) models and quantum convolutional neural networks (QCNN) have demonstrated promise in applications like image recognition and malware classification, they remain vulnerable to adversarial threats. Inspired by the vulnerabilities in classical ML, this work systematically investigates how attacks-ranging from training-time poisoning and inferencetime evasion (e.g., quantum versions of FGSM and PGD) to deployment-time trigger attacks-manifest in quantum circuits. We explore how classical adversarial strategies adapt to quantum models through parameterized encodings, hybrid architectures, and probabilistic outputs. The poster evaluates different quantum classifiers under adversarial settings, comparing their robustness, trainability, and resilience to perturbations. Defense mechanisms such as randomized encoding, engineered noise channels, and quantum adversarial training are analyzed in relation to specific threat types. In addition, we assess how quantum models behave differently than classical counterparts under attack, and how quantum-specific design constraints can either exacerbate or mitigate these effects. Our ultimate goal is to define and construct robust QML systems that are both theoretically sound and practically secure. By synthesizing results across multiple QML architectures and threat models, this work provides actionable insight into the development of resilient quantum classifiers, paving the way toward trustworthy and scalable quantum machine learning.


