N2  - Cloud infrastructure is evolving toward increased dynamism, exhibiting volatile workloads and diverse virtual machines. Within this context, multicriteria task scheduling that maximizes efficacy in Infrastructure-as-a-Service (IaaS) remains a pervasive, unresolved challenge. Contemporary heuristic and deterministic algorithms struggle to optimally reconcile simultaneous objectives—namely, energy consumption, resource utilization, and makespan. Therefore, the introduction of intelligent, adaptive scheduling frameworks is vital for delivering sustained operational efficiency. We propose a novel multi-objective task mapping scheme, designated QCoNNet-SBirdO, in which a secretary-bird species-inspired optimizer directs a quantum convolutional neural network to schedule cloud workloads. The method commences with comprehensive environment profiling—collecting task dependency matrices, utilization metrics, and power consumption at the hypervisor layer. A priority ranking for both task and virtual-machine (VM) candidates is deduced, and this ranking feeds a Quantum Convolutional Neural Network (QCoNNet) that exploits multidimensional resource interactions. Delegated task-to-VM mapping is subsequently derived via Secretary Bird Optimization (SBirdO), which iteratively refines conductivity and depth measurements until scheduling objectives converge. Outcome allocations undergo a reinforcement-control loop to ingest real-time discrepancies and augment fidelity. Empirical evaluations across heterogeneously provisioned cloud environments indicate that QCoNNet-SBirdO achieves a task prioritization accuracy of 99.2%, a reduction of 38.6% in energy consumption, a decrease of 41.3% in allocated resource requirement, and a 99.7% improvement in mapping velocity when benchmarked against reference heuristics. The results thus corroborate that the QCoNNet-SBirdO framework is both scalable and, in multicriteria terms, decisively superior to contemporary deterministic and meta-heuristic benchmarks across pivotal operational metrics.


