N2  - Lung cancer is the leading cause of cancer-related mortality in the world and thus outlines the need to discover diagnostic methods beneficial to improve patient survival. Accurate classification of pathological images is essential for sub-typing and as a means for guiding therapy. The current research work presents latent equivariant quantum convolutional neural network (LEQCNN) with improved orca predation algorithm (IOPA) (Latent Equivariant Quantum Convolutional Neural Network augmented with Improved Orca Predation Algorithm or LEqCNN-IoPa) for automating the diagnostic process of lung cancer. Initially, initial stage preprocessing for the LC25000 dataset of histopathological images has been conducted using the Window-Aware Guided Image Filtering (WAGIF) technique to improve structural integrity, and also to reduce artefacts from noise. The improvement is further obtained via segmentation using a Return-(a)-ligned Decision Transformer (RADT) that captures the contextual relationship on pixel level further. The architecture is further enhanced to include Feedback DenseNet201 (FDNet201) which integrates feedback attention with dense connectivity to further improve the saliency of clinically meaningful features in the network. Finally, hyper-parameter optimisation is done by the IOPA meta-hyperparameter algorithm, which is based on biologically inspired exploration-exploitation mechanisms. Experimental results show that the LEQCNN-IOPA can achieve the overall precision of 99.85%, accuracy of 99.62%, and sensitivity of 99.59%.


