N2  - Autonomous driving systems demand real-time processing of high-dimensional sensor data, large-scale decision making, NP-hard routing optimization under strict latency and computational constraints. Classical approaches using convolutional neural networks face scalability issues and performance limitations in dynamic environments. IBM's Qiskit framework provides a comprehensive solution with a modular architecture, enabling the construction of quantum circuits that encode multi-sensor data into exponentially large Hilbert spaces. The core components like Terra and Aer support circuit design and noise-aware simulation. Additional libraries extend these capabilities to support quantum machine learning and optimization algorithms. This paper presents a unified Qiskit-based framework incorporating quantum neural networks for trajectory prediction, quantum convolutional neural networks for safety classification in poor conditions, and quantum approximate optimization algorithm for efficient vehicle routing. We showcase how quantum superposition explores multiple routing paths in parallel and how quantum circuits represent grid locations to improve path finding. Our survey reveals measurable performance advantages of quantum-enhanced models over classical methods, forming the basis of quantum-accelerated autonomous driving systems.


