N2  - Advancements in quantum computing have sparked a systematic evaluation of quantum machine learning (QML) models, focusing on their behavior and performance across diverse learning tasks such as classification, regression, generative modeling, and feature compression. The primary focus is on assessing the performance and behavior of various QML architectures-including Quantum Neural Networks (QNNs), Quantum Support Vector Classifiers (QSVCs), Quantum Generative Adversarial Networks (QGANs), Quantum Convolutional Neural Networks (QCNNs), and Quantum Autoencoders (QAEs)-implemented using Qiskit's machine learning library. This evaluation is conducted using an existing benchmarking framework that ensures consistent experimental conditions and supports flexible evaluation strategies. To provide additional context and insight, these quantum models are compared against classical counterparts using widely adopted implementations. Experiments are carried out on a range of datasets, including structured and low-dimensional examples like Bars and Stripes, as well as more complex datasets like Downscaled MNIST and synthetic Hyperplanes. By providing a standardized evaluation of these models within the Qiskit framework, this study establishes a reproducible performance baseline and highlights key challenges for QML systems in the noisy intermediate-scale quantum (NISQ) era.


