N2  - To address electricity theft in smart meters within smart grids, this paper proposes a theft detection model based on a one-dimensional quantum convolutional neural network (1D-QCNN) to identify anomalous behaviors in users' electricity consumption data. The proposed model comprises a quantum encoding layer, alternately arranged quantum convolution and pooling layers, and a classical fully connected layer. By leveraging the parallelism of quantum computing and the feature extraction capabilities of convolutional neural networks, the model effectively captures the nonlinear characteristics of electricity theft behavior. Experiments conducted on the Irish Smart Energy Trial dataset demonstrate that, under the scenario of daily electricity consumption per user, the model achieves a detection accuracy of 87.07 % while maintaining a lightweight architecture. These results indicate strong potential and effectiveness of the model in electricity theft detection tasks.


