N2  - Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum (NISQ) devices have led to the creation of hybrid quantum-classical algorithms, which have provided an innovative design to improve classical machine learning. This article focuses on a review of Hybrid Quantum-Classical Neural Networks (H-QCNNs) in image classification, which is an emerging area that attempts to exploit the representational ability of parameterized quantum circuits (PQCs) and the proven feature extraction capabilities of classical deep learning. We empirically examine the current tendencies in architecture, such as quanvolutional networks, sequential models, and variational quantum classifiers, and critically assess their performance on standard and custom data. The literature analysis shows a large performance difference between noiseless simulation and execution on real hardware, indicating the highly problematic aspects of scaling beyond binary tasks, quantum noise, and a lack of standardized benchmarking. Moreover, we mention such innovative contributions as the small H-QNN model that offers comparable accuracy, yet meets strict NISQ requirements. Last but not least, the current review offers a systematic taxonomy of H-QCNN architectures, a comparative study of methodology options, and a clear roadmap to future research to reach a workable quantum advantage in computer vision. It is a resource to the beginners as well as a reference to the professionals working in the field, so the field would be moved to more powerful, scalable, and hardware-conscious ways of implementation.


