N2  - The intricate architecture of the Central Nervous System (CNS) underscores its pivotal role in sensory and motor function, with the brain's diverse regions—cerebellum, brainstem, and cerebrum—each contributing uniquely to cognitive and physiological processes. This comparative study delves into the critical challenges of brain tumor and ischemic stroke segmentation, emphasizing the necessity for precise imaging techniques, particularly MRI, in diagnosing and treating these conditions. We explore the evolution of segmentation methodologies, from traditional active contour models to cutting-edge quantum machine learning (QML) approaches, highlighting their respective strengths and limitations. Our analysis reveals significant advancements in segmentation accuracy and computational efficiency, particularly through hybrid quantum-classical frameworks that leverage the power of Quantum Convolutional Neural Networks (QCNNs) and advanced deep learning techniques. By synthesizing findings from recent literature, we demonstrate how these innovative algorithms not only enhance diagnostic precision but also pave the way for personalized treatment strategies. This study ultimately advocates for the integration of quantum computing in medical imaging, positing that such advancements could revolutionize brain tumor management and improve patient outcomes. Future research directions are proposed to optimize these models for broader clinical applicability, ensuring robust performance across diverse imaging datasets and real-world scenarios.


