N2  - Motivated by the anticipated advantage of quantum computing in complex tasks, quantum machine learning (QML) combines quantum principles with machine learning (ML) for potential speedups. This paper reviews recent progress in three main directions: (a) Variational Quantum Algorithms (VQAs), (b) Quantum Convolutional Neural Networks (QCNNs), and (c) quantum kernel methods. Key challenges are highlighted, including data encoding, hardware noise in the Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum (NISQ) regime, and the importance of standardized benchmarking. Promising strategies to address these challenges include hybrid quantum-classical integration, transfer learning, and algorithm-hardware co-design. Experimental demonstrations on platforms developed by IBM, Google, and IonQ illustrate the feasibility of QML on near-term quantum devices. Looking forward, advances in encoding schemes, noise-resilient circuit design, and domain-specific applications in chemistry, materials, and biomedicine are expected to further accelerate the practical development of QML.


