N2  - The main limitations of agricultural productivity are diseases and shortage of micro-nutrients especially in vegetables like bitter gourd. It is of essence that such stresses are detected early in an attempt to maximize the yield so that farming activities can find a place within the sustainability of the environment. The dataset that we have used in this paper is the image dataset of an olive leaf namely OLID-I subset of bitter gourd leaves that has a total of 785 high-resolution images divided into nine different classes, namely, healthy, disease, insect attack and nutrient deficiencies. Note, as the baseline, we use AgriCure-YOLOv8 model, trained on layered augmentations, with reported good performance of mAP score of 92.9% and a precision of 89.6% and a recall of 86.6%. Therefore, in our work we have QML algorithms in mind on leaf stress classification and using the result offered by the YOLOv8 baseline as the starting point we carry out scale-up analysis. Three models are compared and contrasted; the Quantum Convolutional Neural Network (QCNN) which is a Hierarchical Quantum Feature Extraction, the Variational Quantum Classifier (VQC) which is a direct quantum classification and a Hybrid Quantum-Classical Classifier (HQ-CC), in which the classical pre-classification is performed before the quantum classification. The obtained findings indicate that the quantum models are workable and can be deployed to handle small but complicated agronomic data and yield benefits in the realms of generalization performance and feature representations. In comparison to the YOLOv8 baseline accuracy, the paper presents quantum models, where the additional performance is demonstrated on identifying nutrient deficiencies, which is the most significant outcome that will be utilized in entering this new paradigm technology of smart agriculture, and in resource-poor environments, in particular, where plant stress is of paramount importance.In contrast to the current deep learning-based systems like the YOLOv8, the proposed quantum models have a higher generalization capability based on small and complex dataset and higher sensitivity to detect nutrient deficiencies. This means that QML is applicable in a resource-constrained agricultural setting.


