N2  - Hybrid Quantum-Classical Convolutional Neural Networks (HQCNNs) have emerged as promising models for learning under low-data regimes, particularly in vision tasks. Despite increasing interest, their noise robustness under realistic quantum conditions remains underexplored. In this study, we propose a hybrid architecture that integrates a quantum convolutional feature extractor and a variational quantum classifier into a classical CNN pipeline for multi-class road scene classification. Using the KITTI dataset with three categories (uu, um, umm), we evaluate the model under both ideal and noisy quantum simulations, introducing depolarizing and thermal noise. The results demonstrate that while quantum modules are susceptible to noise, the hybrid structure retains competitive performance under moderate noise and effectively leverages quantum features in small-data settings. Our findings highlight the potential and current limitations of HQCNNs for robust visual classification in the NISQ era.


