N2  - The growing prospect of incorporating artificial intelligence into medical diagnosis to enhance the healthcare system necessitates methods to improve the accuracy of classification models capable of processing complex information, such as computed tomography images. Quantum machine learning has garnered attention because of the superior computational capacity of qubits, which may lead to improved pattern recognition and data analysis. However, there is limited information on its ability to process 3D computed tomography volumes, especially from real-world datasets such as the RSNA Abdominal Trauma Injury CT (RATIC) Dataset that present data with more variability and irrelevant details. This study addresses this by proposing four HQCNN MIL-based model variations and evaluating them against a baseline classical model to determine an optimal classification model architecture for the RATIC Dataset. The model is part of a comprehensive data processing pipeline, which involves a 3D CNN image segmentation model that has been modified to increase dimensionality and to incorporate a ResNet encoder. While the performance of two HQCNNs is similar to the baseline, that of the other two is consistently higher, with score differences of approximately 0.06 to 0.13. The results indicate that even with 3D CT data, hybrid machine learning models have the potential to surpass purely classical configurations only when optimally designed, suggesting that careful integration of quantum methods into machine learning pipelines plays a key role in advancing diagnostic accuracy and reliability in clinical practice. Overall, these findings highlight the need for continued exploration of hybrid architectures, particularly in optimizing quantum-classical interactions for robust performance on real-world medical data.


