N2  - Brain tumor detection from MRI images is a critical and challenging task in medical diagnostics, where early identification greatly improves treatment outcomes. This paper presents a Hybrid Quantum– Classical Convolutional Neural Network (HQCNN) that integrates the strengths of ResNet-18 and a Variational Quantum Circuit for efficient and accurate tumor classification. The proposed model first employs ResNet-18 as a classical feature extractor, where 17 convolutional layers and pooling operations capture both low-level and high-level spatial features from MRI images. The final fully connected layer of ResNet-18 is replaced by a quantum processing layer implemented using PennyLane. In this layer, the extracted classical features are encoded onto qubits using RY rotation gates, while CNOT gates introduce entanglement between them, forming a VQC with trainable parameters. After quantum computation, Pauli-Z measurements convert the processed quantum states back into classical values, which are passed to a final fully connected layer for binary classification as Tumor or No Tumor. The hybrid integration of classical CNN feature learning with quantum entanglement– based representation enhances model generalization and interpretability. Experimental analysis demonstrates that the proposed HQCNN achieved 94.65% accuracy, outperforming traditional CNN models and confirming the potential of hybrid quantum–classical architectures for reliable and interpretable medical image diagnosis.


