N2  - Quantum circuits and classical convolutional neural networks are used in hybrid quantum-classical neural networks (HQCNNs) to classify medical images, particularly MRI and CT scans for bone cancer diagnosis. This review covers cutting-edge hybrid designs for parallel quantum circuit tumour detection, illness diagnosis, and anatomical pattern identification. Recent research reveal methodological trends, performance benchmarks, and architectural advances that improve classification accuracy, computing efficiency, and parameter optimisation by quantum. On medical imaging datasets like musculoskeletal imaging, parallel quantum computing architectures train 12 times quicker while keeping accuracy. Quantum hardware limits, noise resilience, scalability restrictions, and model interpretability are discussed in relation to clinical bone cancer screening implementation. Quantum transfer learning, federated quantum machine learning, and orthopaedic imaging-optimized hardware-aware quantum circuit design are future directions.


