N2  - The emergence of Beyond 5G (B5G) and 6G networks introduces advanced capabilities in speed, latency, and connectivity, while also amplifying the risk of sophisticated security threats such as False Base Stations (FBS). These rogue entities imitate legitimate base stations to intercept data, perform man-in-the-middle attacks, or disrupt services. Researchers have explored conventional machine learning methods for FBS detection, but these methods often struggle to address the high-dimensional and dynamic nature of next-generation networks. This paper proposes a Quanvolutional Neural Network (QNN) framework as a quantum-enhanced solution for FBS detection. QNN utilizes quantum phenomena such as superposition and entanglement to perform complex feature transformations, enabling more expressive data representations than classical models. We evaluate the proposed QNN against a traditional Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) using a classification task designed to simulate the detection of FBS. Results demonstrate that although the QNN exhibits slower convergence during early training, it ultimately outperforms the CNN across all key evaluation metrics. The QNN achieves higher accuracy (90.65%), improved recall, lower false positive rate, and a superior F1 score, while also attaining a lower final training loss. These findings suggest that QNNs have strong potential for real-time, localized threat detection in AInative 6G architectures. Future work will explore deployment on Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum (NISQ) hardware and evaluate model performance under realistic noise conditions. This study contributes to ongoing research on integrating quantum machine learning into secure and intelligent wireless communication systems


