N2  - In this paper, Quantum Neural Networks are the significant development in connecting quantum computing with artificial intelligence using superposition and entanglement principles within the architecture of a neural network. Currently, quantum devices come into the category of Noisy Intermediate-Scale Quantum and are strictly bound by the presence of noise and decoherence and the limited number of qubits, which directly affects QNN performance and scalability. This paper reviews existing QNN architectures, training methodologies, and optimization techniques relevant for NISQ hardware. Various approaches have been analyzed, including VQCs, QCNNs, and QBMs, based on their feasibility of implementation, computational efficiency, and robustness against quantum noise. Furthermore, this survey covers hybrid classical-quantum optimization algorithms, gradientbased learning techniques, and noise mitigation frameworks that constitute the practical basis for performance improvement in QNNs. It underlines the challenges to be overcome and discusses open problems and possible future directions toward scalable and fault-tolerant quantum neural network systems for nextgeneration quantum computing platforms.


