N2  - Brain Tumor detection knows as tedious job poses huge intricacies. Though MRI(Magnetic Resonance Imaging) has made the life of clinicians easier by providing detailed cross-sections of the brain, thus making it easy to detect abnormalities, it is still very difficult to accurately categorize the different types of Brain Tumors. For addressing this problem, this research work proposes a novel approach to use a Hybrid Quantum Classical Convolutional Neural Network (HQC-CNN) model. The proposed model has been experimented on the BraTS2023 dataset, with a result of 98.7% accuracy, which is an improvement of 5.2% over traditional CNNs (ResNet, VGG). The HQC-CNN model simplifies parameter complexity by 40% and training time by 2.5 times via quantum parallelism. The complete flow is described in the architecture, hybrid training regimes, and issues in deploying QCNNs on near-term quantum hardware in this work. These findings strongly indicates the power of Deep Learning approaches and incorporation of Quantum ideas in medical imaging.


